  "notes": "EVENT: Intel ISEF 2017, Los Angeles Convention Center, Los Angeles, California, May 14-19, 2017 (awards ceremony Friday May 19). Society for Science & the Public press materials say 'nearly 2,000' / 'approximately 1,778' finalists from 78 countries, regions and territories; the USC Viterbi blog says 'over 1,775 students from 78 countries'; the Society program book says 'more than 1,750'. Keep 1,778 / 78 as the headline figures. Approximately 600 finalists received awards; ~US$4 million total awarded. TOP AWARDS: Gordon E. Moore Award $75,000 to Ivo Zell (18, Lorch/Hessen, Germany, Engineering Mechanics); two Intel Foundation Young Scientist Awards $50,000 each to Amber Yang (18, Windermere FL, Physics and Astronomy) and Valerio Pagliarino (17, Castelnuovo Calcea, Italy, Embedded Systems); together $175,000. The three top winners were also Best of Category winners in their own categories. No Craig R. Barrett, Horvitz, Peggy Scripps, Yancopoulos or Mary Sue Coleman awards existed in 2017 (those were introduced 2019+). BEST OF CATEGORY: the official press release says 22 Best of Category winners, each US$5,000 plus a US$1,000 Intel Foundation grant to the winner's school and to the affiliated fair; one search summary rendered this as '20' -- that is a summarization error, 22 categories/22 winners were in use in 2017 (Energy was still split into Energy: Chemical and Energy: Physical; Embedded Systems; Engineering Mechanics; no Technology Enhances the Arts yet). All 22 categories are filled above; none missing. Every Best of Category winner also received the First Award of $3,000 in their category. SIYSS: three Dudley R. Herschbach SIYSS Award winners in 2017 (Karina Movsesian, Jeremiah Pate, Prathik Naidu) per the Society blog 'Teen cancer researchers meet Nobel Laureates in Stockholm'; one search summary listed only two (Movsesian, Pate). EUCYS: two EU Contest for Young Scientists Award winners (Kendra Zhang, Nicky Wojtania) travelled to EUCYS 2017 in Tallinn, Estonia; Zhang won the Danone special award there. Also noted: Intel Foundation Cultural and Scientific Visit to China Award (CASTIC) went to five finalists including Erin Smith, Karthik Yegnesh and Tassilo Schwarz (other two not captured). DISAGREEMENTS/CAVEATS: (1) Prashaant Ranganathan's title appears in two forms -- short 'Biodegradation of Chlorpyrifos using native bacteria' (Indian press) vs. the official project code ENEV074 'Biodegradation of Chlorpyrifos and Soil Remediation Using Native Soil Bacteria and Triton X - A Novel Approach'; the long form is kept as the ISEF title. Indian press spells his name 'Prashant'; Society spelling 'Prashaant' kept. (2) Arnob Das: ACS community grand-awards page lists him as 2017 Chemistry First Award ($3,000); other web bios mention a Materials Science second award and a 2015 Chemistry third award -- likely different years, both noted. (3) Some search summaries mixed in other years' winners (e.g., Ethan Levy and Tanishka Aglave are 2018 winners; Michelle Marquez, Abhishek Verma/Daksh Dua, Garyk Brixi are 2018/2019; Michael Shao is 2015) -- these were excluded. (4) Karthik Yegnesh's city not confirmed from an ISEF source; Methacton High School is in Eagleville (Lower Providence Township), PA. (5) Karina Movsesian's school name is rendered without diacritics as in the Society press release ('Prvni Ceske Gymnazium v Karlovych Varech' = Prvni ceske gymnazium v Karlovych Varech). first_awards and second_awards are intentionally partial (only entries seen explicitly in search content); the full category-by-category grand-award list (First $3,000 / Second $1,500 / Third $1,000 / Fourth $500) is in the Society's 2017 Grand Award press release, which could not be fetched directly (WebFetch blocked).",
